v 3
d 'GEPWREG': module for percentile weights regression: the effect of the covariates at a quantile of the outcome
d
d  gepwreg estimates the effect of the covariates on an outcome for the
d  units located at a target quantile of the outcome, at the initial quantile
d  (the outcome before the contribution of chosen covariates), or along the
d  rank of an observed covariate, with the percentile weights of Araar (2016):
d  Gaussian kernel weights on the percentile scale. The effect at a quantile of
d  the outcome is estimated in two steps: unit-level effects from a
d  heterogeneity model fitted on the whole sample (a conditional quantile
d  regression, or interactions with observed variables) are averaged with the
d  percentile weights on the rank of the outcome, and the estimator converges
d  to the unconditional quantile partial effect. Standard errors come from the
d  influence function of the whole procedure, with Taylor linearisation under a
d  complex survey design declared by svyset, or from a bootstrap that re-runs
d  the whole procedure; the bandwidth is the MSE-optimal plug-in. ref() gives
d  the effect for a given household, which holds the composition of the group
d  at the quantile fixed, and graph() draws the profile of the effects across
d  percentiles with a simultaneous confidence band and a test that the effect
d  is constant across percentiles. merr corrects the interaction model for
d  classical measurement error in the heterogeneity variables. gepwreg_setable
d  compares the standard errors; a dialog box is included, and the examples of
d  the help run from clickable links (gepwreg_examples). The paper that
d  documents the estimators is included as an ancillary file
d  (gepwreg_paper.pdf) and on Zenodo: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22845707
d
d KW: percentile weights regression
d KW: unconditional quantile partial effect
d KW: quantile regression
d KW: heterogeneous effects
d KW: survey design
d KW: influence function
d KW: bandwidth selection
d
d Requires: Stata version 16
d
d Distribution-Date: 20261005
d
d Author: Abdelkrim Araar, Universite Laval and Partnership for Economic Policy (PEP)
d Support: email aabd@ecn.ulaval.ca
d
